    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe endpoint-shape choices are idiomatic and fit the codebase: a dedicated `ValidateOwnerPostcode` step mirrors the existing `ValidateOwnerFields` guard, the checked `InvalidPostcodeException` + `officefloor/escalation/...yml` + `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` trio matches the established per-concern exception/handler convention (cf. `InvalidEmailException`, `InvalidTelephoneException`), reusing `OwnerFieldErrors` for the 400 body is consistent, and the wiring/schema/openapi additions are correctly placed. The single \"validate only when present\" method is cohesive and sits in the right unit.\n\nThe problem is duplicated domain knowledge:\n\n- **The city\u2192region table is now defined twice.** `ValidateOwnerPostcode.CITY_REGION` (`Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD`) is a verbatim copy of the table already encoded in `OwnerMapper.locality` (`src/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java:78-93`). The new class's own javadoc admits it \"mirrors `OwnerMapper.locality`.\" These are the write-time and read-time sides of the *same* rule, so they must agree \u2014 but a future city addition has to be made in two unrelated places (a MapStruct mapper and a validation function) and will silently drift otherwise.\n\n- **This codebase already has the idiom for exactly this situation.** `OwnerAddress` (`src/.../function/owner/OwnerAddress.java`) is documented as the \"single source of truth\" for a normalisation rule shared between `ValidateOwnerFields` (write-time) and `OwnerHousehold` (read/compare-time). The city\u2192region table is the same shape of shared rule and should follow the same pattern.\n\nConcrete fix: extract the city\u2192region table into one shared helper (e.g. an `OwnerLocality` final class with a `regionFor(String city)` method, alongside `OwnerAddress`), and have both `OwnerMapper.locality` and `ValidateOwnerPostcode` call it. Reconcile the two \"no known region\" encodings while doing so \u2014 the mapper returns the sentinel `\"UNKNOWN\"` whereas the validator treats `null` as \"no region\"; the shared helper should define one contract and each caller adapt (the validator wants \"no range \u2192 accept any 4-digit\"). The postcode-range table can stay in the validator since the mapper doesn't need it.",
